WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.360
NUANCE ACQUISITION:
On March 4, 2022, Microsoft announced that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:10.080
had completed a $19.7 billion cash acquisition,&nbsp;
Microsoft’s second largest acquisition of all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:16.400
time. What company did they buy you ask? Maybe&nbsp;
LinkedIn, GitHub, or Activision Blizzard? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:22.320
it was actually a company that you’ve probably&nbsp;
never heard of: Nuance Communications. On the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:27.760
surface, Nuance is a speech recognition and speech&nbsp;
synthesis company. So you would naturally think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:33.320
that Nuance is some sort of AI based voice&nbsp;
assistant play. Something like Siri or Alexa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:40.160
probably to make Cortana better. But that wasn’t&nbsp;
why Microsoft bought Nuance. Well then, maybe they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:45.800
bought Nuance as a chatbot play. Speech synthesis&nbsp;
technology could be a massive help for ChatGPT to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:52.200
form natural sentences and paragraphs. But that&nbsp;
wasn’t why Microsoft bought Nuance either. Well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:57.680
then, in that case, Nuance must have been a market&nbsp;
share play right? Nuance must have controlled a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:02.800
massive portion of the market within the speech&nbsp;
technology world. Well, that’s partially true in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:09.200
that Nuance was indeed a market share play but not&nbsp;
in the way that you might expect. Nuance didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:13.320
have massive market share against any of the&nbsp;
traditional players whether it be Google assistant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:19.320
or ChatGPT. In fact, Nuance doesn’t have all that&nbsp;
much to do with the traditional tech industry in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:24.920
general. But then again, it’s not like Microsoft&nbsp;
needs that much help in this area anyway. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:29.920
that’s why the Nuance deal was actually about&nbsp;
breaking into a completely different industry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:35.160
one that Microsoft didn’t control: the healthcare&nbsp;
industry. You see, over the past few decades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:40.800
Nuance has built a reputation as the go to&nbsp;
platform for hospitals and physicians. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:48.640
55% of physicians, 75% of radiologists, and 77%&nbsp;
of American hospitals use Nuance solutions. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:53.560
by purchasing Nuance, Microsoft immediately&nbsp;
became the dominant player within the healthcare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:59.840
industry as well. But all of that is just the&nbsp;
beginning as Microsoft has much grander ambitions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:12.423
with Nuance. So, here’s why Microsoft really&nbsp;
spent $20 billion to buy a no name company.

00:02:12.423 --> 00:02:13.880
NOBEL BEGINNINGS:
A lot of us probably discount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:19.160
the healthcare sector because it’s not exactly the&nbsp;
most shiny or glamorous. They don’t have massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:24.120
product launches like Apple or Google nor do&nbsp;
they have the same brand or reputation. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:28.560
the healthcare industry is one of those&nbsp;
background giants that’s actually extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:34.240
powerful. In fact, even more powerful than the&nbsp;
tech industry. In the US for example, healthcare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:41.400
accounts for over 18% of the national GDP or&nbsp;
about $4.3 trillion per year. For perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:46.080
the tech industry is only half as large,&nbsp;
accounting for only 9.3% of the American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:52.120
GDP or just under $2 trillion. But, not only&nbsp;
is the healthcare industry already massive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:57.760
but they’re expected to continue growing at a&nbsp;
record pace of 5.4% over the next decade. Tech on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:03.480
the other hand has actually been on a decline in&nbsp;
terms fo GDP contribution. In fact, 2022 was lower&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:10.080
than 2017. And given that Microsoft is already one&nbsp;
of the largest players within the tech industry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:15.200
it’s no wonder why they’re trying to expand&nbsp;
their horizons to other massive industries. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:19.400
ironically, Nuance didn’t start off as a&nbsp;
healthcare based tech company or a profit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:24.680
oriented business in general. They actually&nbsp;
trace their roots all the way back to 1974 to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:30.400
a man named Raymond Kurzweil. To be honest, I’ve&nbsp;
never heard of Raymond till now but looking into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:36.360
his background, the man is absolutely stacked, not&nbsp;
in terms of wealth like other people we cover but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:42.040
in terms of contributions. The man is a first&nbsp;
class academic scholar and inventor with dozens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:47.640
and dozens of contributions that companies have&nbsp;
gone on to commercialize to great success. Just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:52.080
to give you a sense of his accomplishments,&nbsp;
Ray has earned 21 honorary doctorates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:58.560
honors from 3 US presidents, and is named as&nbsp;
one of the 16 revolutionaries that made America.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:04.280
Even before he founded the precursor to Nuance&nbsp;
Communications, Ray was already set. It turns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:08.720
out that while he was in college, he managed&nbsp;
to create an application sorting software that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:13.160
could match high school students with colleges.&nbsp;
And he would go on to sell this company shortly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:19.520
after for a $100,000 plus royalties which&nbsp;
is the same as $750,000 2020 dollars. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:25.000
it’s safe to say that Ray was financially set by&nbsp;
the time he was in his mid 20s but that didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:30.520
stop him from inventing which brings us into&nbsp;
Kurzweil Computer Products Inc in 1974. Ray’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:35.080
goal with this company was to create the world’s&nbsp;
first omni-font optical character recognition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:39.440
software. You’re probably familiar with Apple’s&nbsp;
live text feature that they launched a few years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:45.320
Live text allows you to copy text from images.&nbsp;
Well, this is precisely the technology that Ray&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:51.080
was developing only 50 years ago, and he would&nbsp;
succeed within just a year actually, but there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:58.080
was one major problem. Ray had no obvious way to&nbsp;
monetize this invention. Worse, he was focusing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:02.840
on applications that weren’t all the lucrative&nbsp;
though they were noble. Their first application&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:07.360
for example was to create the world’s first print&nbsp;
to speech reading machines for the blind. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:11.520
this invention did earn them some notoriety on&nbsp;
the news, it didn’t provide them with all that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:18.120
much revenue. In fact, Ray admits that by 1978,&nbsp;
they were scrambling to raise funds. And as such,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:22.360
they would pivot into more commercial applications&nbsp;
of this technology such as scanning documents into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:28.680
databases. This endeavor would catch the attention&nbsp;
of the tech leader of the time: Xerox. Xerox had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:33.880
spent decades figuring out how to convert digital&nbsp;
text into physical text and this startup had built&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:39.600
a bridge backwards. This technology obviously&nbsp;
fit perfectly into Xerox’ arsenal, so they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:51.785
would buy the company in 1980 for a modest $3.6&nbsp;
million, but all of this was just the beginning.

00:05:51.785 --> 00:05:53.600
A GLOBAL GIANT:
The Xerox acquisition didn’t really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:59.120
change anything about Kurzweil Computer Products&nbsp;
because Xerox was a pretty chill company. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.720 --> 00:06:04.400
probably way to chill for their own good. If&nbsp;
you didn’t know, Xerox basically missed out on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:08.920
multiple hundred billion dollars opportunities&nbsp;
that they created from laptops and computers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:14.240
to Microsoft Office and object oriented&nbsp;
programming. Why you ask? Well, Xerox&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:18.280
was never all that concerned about protecting&nbsp;
their intellectual property or commercializing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.280 --> 00:06:23.680
these insane inventions largely because they were&nbsp;
overconfident in their photocopying business which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:28.560
eventually got destroyed. But, while this mindset&nbsp;
wasn’t all that great for Xerox as a whole,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:33.080
it was great for Kurzweil. He basically had&nbsp;
the freedom to experiment with whatever he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:39.080
wanted and Xerox would bankroll his efforts. And&nbsp;
let’s just say, Ray took full advantage of this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.080 --> 00:06:43.480
Instead of focusing on commercial applications,&nbsp;
Ray would revert to focusing on text to speech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.480 --> 00:06:48.680
for the blind. He would implement compatibility&nbsp;
for several languages including French, German,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.680 --> 00:06:53.600
Italian, Spanish, and Swedish. And he would&nbsp;
package all this technology into a streamlined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:58.920
product: the Kurzweil Personal Reader which&nbsp;
launched in April of 1988. The Kurzweil Personal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:06.040
Reader was by no means cheap coming in at $8000&nbsp;
to $12000 but that was simply how much it cost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.040 --> 00:07:10.680
to build such technology back in the 80s. As&nbsp;
Windows computers started to gain popularity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:16.520
Ray would bring this technology to Windows through&nbsp;
a piece of software called Textbridge. Fun fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:21.320
this software is still available on Xerox’s&nbsp;
website to this day, though it hasn’t been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.320 --> 00:07:27.240
updated in nearly 20 years. But while Textbridge&nbsp;
lives on to this day, Ray wouldn’t stick around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:33.160
at Xerox for much longer. In 1995, Ray would&nbsp;
leave to create Kurzweil Educational Systems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.160 --> 00:07:38.800
which focused on helping people with dyslexia and&nbsp;
ADHD. But while Ray was no longer involved in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:43.400
picture, Kurzweil Computer Products would&nbsp;
continue to evolve and grow. Under Xerox,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:48.520
it was known as Xerox Imaging Systems before&nbsp;
becoming ScanSoft. Honestly, it would’ve been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.520 --> 00:07:54.320
in Xerox’s best interest to hold onto ScanSoft&nbsp;
for as long as possible. But, let’s not forget,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.320 --> 00:07:58.800
these are the same people that turned around&nbsp;
and sold their 5-8% stake Apple for just $1.2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:04.200
million. So, it’s not all the surprising that&nbsp;
Xerox dropped the ball with ScanSoft as well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:09.560
selling them off to Visioneer in 1998. From there,&nbsp;
Scansoft would engage in a series of acquisitions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.560 --> 00:08:14.760
and mergers to expand their product portfolio&nbsp;
from just text recognition to speech recognition.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:21.120
Likely the most notable of these acquisitions&nbsp;
was Nuance for $220 million in 2005. Nuance was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:27.000
a relatively new company that had launched a hit&nbsp;
product named Dragon NaturallySpeaking in 1997.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:31.440
As the name suggests, Dragon NaturallySpeaking was&nbsp;
focused on deciphering voice commands, speech to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:36.760
text, and text to speech. Technically, Dragon is&nbsp;
a Windows software that basically anyone can get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:41.400
if they want. But, for the most part, Dragon&nbsp;
was something that was recommended by doctors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:46.440
and healthcare providers to people with various&nbsp;
disabilities. When ScanSoft acquired Nuance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.440 --> 00:08:56.260
they decided to take on their name leading&nbsp;
to the creation of Nuance Communications.

00:08:56.260 --> 00:08:58.440
MICROSOFT TAKESOVER:
Over the years, Nuance managed to grow pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.440 --> 00:09:04.000
large in terms of marketcap but their vision and&nbsp;
purpose was never really clouded. I mean, despite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:08.760
being in the extremely lucrative industry of&nbsp;
healthcare and controlling 77% of the market,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:13.920
Nuance never really made all that much money&nbsp;
themselves. Even at their peak, they were pulling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:18.440
in $2 billion. For perspective, United Health&nbsp;
Group, the largest health insurance provider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.440 --> 00:09:24.760
in America pulls in $324 billion per year. So, it&nbsp;
turns out that the company that’s supposed to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:29.200
protecting Americans from large medical bills is&nbsp;
pulling in hundreds of billions per year while the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:34.600
company actually helping people with disabilities&nbsp;
is only pulling in 2 billion, and that’s just from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.600 --> 00:09:39.280
a revenue perspective. If you look at net income,&nbsp;
you’ll see that Nuance has basically never been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.280 --> 00:09:44.760
consistently profitable. They’ve maintained more&nbsp;
of a breakeven financial profile earning or losing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:50.680
$100 to $200 million in an given year. And this&nbsp;
is precisely why so many people are worried about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:56.600
the Microsoft acquisiton. Nuance has historically&nbsp;
been a company with very noble intentions. Wealth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.600 --> 00:10:01.800
was something that always followed for them, not&nbsp;
something that led, and people are worried that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:06.480
this will change with Microsoft’s influence.&nbsp;
I mean, there’s no question that Microsoft’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:11.480
intentions with Nuance are very opportunistic.&nbsp;
They basically saw that this company was killing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.480 --> 00:10:16.520
it within the healthcare industry, something&nbsp;
that most tech companies had no control over. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.520 --> 00:10:21.240
they went ahead and bought Nuance not to control&nbsp;
speech recognition or whatever but to win&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:26.920
influence over the healthcare industry. Over the&nbsp;
past 50 years, Nuance has built deep long lasting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.920 --> 00:10:32.360
relationships with thousands of hospitals and&nbsp;
hundreds of thousands of doctors. This gives them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.360 --> 00:10:37.160
the perfect opportunity to use these connections&nbsp;
to push Microsoft Azure to the entire healthcare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.160 --> 00:10:42.120
industry. And given that Microsoft has an entire&nbsp;
website about how Microsoft Cloud can be used for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.120 --> 00:10:47.960
healthcare, it does seem like this is their real&nbsp;
angle with Nuance. With that being said though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.960 --> 00:10:52.360
Microsoft does have a history of being very&nbsp;
lenient with their acquisitions. Whether it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.360 --> 00:10:57.400
LinkedIn or GitHub, Microsoft pretty much gives&nbsp;
them full reign of how to run their companies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.400 --> 00:11:02.440
In fact, it’s almost as if these companies&nbsp;
are completely independent of Microsoft. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.440 --> 00:11:06.760
given that Nuance’s CEO, Mark Benjamin, has&nbsp;
stuck around, it seems that relations between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.760 --> 00:11:11.920
Nuance and Microsoft are going pretty well. So,&nbsp;
it may be the case that Microsoft is willing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.920 --> 00:11:16.120
let Nuance do whatever they want when it comes to&nbsp;
speech recognition while bankrolling all of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.120 --> 00:11:22.280
operations. All Microsoft wants in return is some&nbsp;
business from Nuance’s healthcare customers. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.280 --> 00:11:28.080
that’s why Microsoft spent $20 billion buying a&nbsp;
no name company. With historically high interest&nbsp;&nbsp;

